In [[chemistry]], a '''mole map''' is a graphical representation of an [[algorithm]] that compares [[molar mass]], number of [[particle]]s per mole, and factors from balanced equations or other formulae.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Kenkel |first=John |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Hu6KBo9cjjcC&pg=PA204 |title=Basic Chemistry Concepts and Exercises |date=2011-07-08 |publisher=CRC Press |isbn=978-1-4398-1338-6 |pages=204 |language=en}}</ref> They are often used in [[Undergraduate education|undergraduate]]-level chemistry courses as a tool to teach the basics of [[stoichiometry]] and [[Conversion of units|unit conversion]].<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=QBEPAQAAMAAJ |title=The Science Teacher |date=2000 |publisher=American Science Teachers Association |volume=67 |pages=38 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-02-14 |title=4.10: Mole Road Map |url=https://chem.libretexts.org/Courses/Madera_Community_College/MacArthur_Chemistry_3A_v_1.2/04%3A_The_Mole_Concept/4.10%3A_Mole_Road_Map |access-date=2024-04-11 |website=Chemistry LibreTexts |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Gilleland |first1=Martha J. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=HjLu5pQge28C |title=Introduction to Chemistry |last2=Williams |first2=Rebecca |date=1986 |publisher=West publishing Company |isbn=978-0-314-96991-0 |pages=106–120 |language=en}}</ref>

