'''Bats''' ([[Order (biology)|order]] '''Chiroptera''' {{IPAc-en|k|aɪ|.|ˈ|r|ɒ|p|.|t|ər|.|ə}}) are [[Bat wing development|wing]]ed mammals, the only [[mammal]]s capable of true and sustained [[Bat flight|flight]]. Bats are more agile in flight than most [[bird]]s, using long, spread-out [[digit (anatomy)|digit]]s covered with a thin membrane or [[patagium]]. The smallest bat, and one of the [[Smallest organisms|smallest extant mammal]]s, is [[Kitti's hog-nosed bat]], which is {{cvt|29|-|33|mm}} in length, {{cvt|150|mm}} across the forearm and {{cvt|2|g}} in mass. The largest bats are the flying foxes, with the [[giant golden-crowned flying fox]] (''Acerodon jubatus'') reaching a weight of {{cvt|1.5|kg}} and having a wingspan of {{cvt|1.6|m|ftin}}.

The second-largest order of mammals after [[rodent]]s, bats account for about 20% of all [[taxonomy (biology)|classified]] mammal [[species]] worldwide, with at least 1,500 known species. These were traditionally divided into two suborders: the largely fruit-eating [[megabat]]s and the [[microbat]]s. However, more recent evidence has supported dividing the order into [[Yinpterochiroptera]] and [[Yangochiroptera]], with megabats as members of the former along with several species of microbats. Many bats are [[insectivore]]s, and most of the rest are [[frugivore]]s (fruit-eaters) or [[nectarivore]]s (nectar-eaters). A few species feed on animals other than insects; for example, the [[vampire bat]]s are [[Hematophagy|haematophagous]] (feed on blood). Most bats are [[nocturnal]], and many roost in caves or other refuges; it is uncertain whether bats have these behaviours to escape [[predator]]s. Bats are distributed globally in almost all regions. They are important in their [[ecosystem]]s for [[pollination|pollinating]] flowers and dispersing seeds as well as controlling insect populations.

Bats provide humans with some direct benefits, at the cost of some disadvantages. Bat dung has been mined as [[guano]] from caves and used as fertiliser. Bats consume insect pests, reducing the need for [[pesticide]]s and other insect management measures. Bats are sometimes numerous enough and close enough to human settlements to serve as tourist attractions, and they are used as food in Africa, Asia, the Pacific and the Caribbean. Due to their [[physiology]], bats are one type of animal that acts as a [[natural reservoir]] of many [[pathogen]]s, such as [[rabies]], and since they are highly mobile, social, and long-lived, they can readily spread disease among themselves. If humans interact with bats, these traits become potentially dangerous to humans.

Depending on the culture, bats may be symbolically associated with positive traits, such as protection from certain diseases or risks, rebirth, or long life, but in [[Western culture|the West]], bats are popularly associated with darkness, malevolence, [[witchcraft]], and [[vampire]]s.

